# Disk-integrity test image: the blockprobe on a real-disk KRFFS.
#
# Built into a partitioned disk by mkdisk.sh and booted with rach2.fsdisk +
# rach2.rescue, so the kernel mounts KRFFS from the disk through the page cache
# and the debug shell runs `exec /blockprobe`. blockprobe writes several files
# whose combined size exceeds the resident cache, fsyncs, reads them back, and
# checks every byte; its "BLOCKPROBE OK" token on the serial is the test result.
file 0755 /blockprobe build/tests/blockprobe

# And procprobe, which `make procfiles-test` runs from the same rescue shell:
# /proc/mounts must name the disk on a boot that mounted one, and this is the
# only image here that does.
file 0755 /procprobe build/tests/procprobe

# The same disk carries dirtyprobe, which `make crash-test` runs from the same
# rescue shell. It proves no set-id clear reaches the root node, whose spare
# mode bit is the dirty-mount flag, and that claim needs a disk: the flag is
# never set on the RAM mount, and stat reports the root with a fixed mode. The
# probe syncs after every attempt so a spurious clear reaches the platter, and
# the offline fsck after the kill is what reads the verdict.
file 0755 /dirtyprobe build/tests/dirtyprobe

# bootprobe proves /proc/cmdline and the two raw disk windows. `make
# bootpart-test` boots this disk three times: once as built (one KRFFS
# partition, so /dev/bootpart must be absent); once with the MBR rewritten by
# tools/mkbootfixture.py to add a FAT-typed partition of known bytes, where the
# device must carry them; and once mounting a RAM image instead of the disk,
# where both /dev/bootpart and /dev/grubcore must be present anyway.
file 0755 /bootprobe build/tests/bootprobe
